  "doc": [
    "*Bombus terrestris*, female, dorsal — the buff-tailed bumblebee.",
    "",
    "Traced from figure 3 of Robert Morgan's plate 52 for Edward Saunders's *The",
    "Hymenoptera Aculeata of the British Islands* (1896), where the caption gives",
    "both the species and the sex. `references/bombus-terrestris.jpg` is the",
    "sheet; the queen is the large figure on the right of the middle row.",
    "",
    "## Three bands, and the last one is the name",
    "",
    "A collar across the front of the thorax, a band across the second segment of",
    "the abdomen, and a pale tail. British bumblebees are keyed out on that",
    "sequence before anything else, and *terrestris* is the one whose tail is buff",
    "rather than white — a distinction this plate cannot make, because the tail is",
    "drawn as bare paper and bare paper is whatever the season's surface token",
    "says it is. The record carries the word `buff-tailed`; the drawing carries",
    "the fact that there is a tail band at all.",
    "",
    "## Hair as hatching",
    "",
    "The whole animal is furred, and fur is the thing an engraving is least able",
    "to copy and most able to *suggest*. Two fans of strokes, one over the thorax",
    "and one over the abdomen, both clipped to the surface they sit on, both at",
    "`detail` weight so they drop out first at thumbnail size and leave a clean",
    "silhouette. Drawing every hair would produce a grey rectangle at eighty",
    "pixels and an unmaintainable file at six hundred.",
    "",
    "## No waist to speak of",
    "",
    "A bumblebee has one — it is a wasp-waisted insect like every aculeate — but",
    "the fur closes over it and nothing of it shows from above. The thorax and the",
    "abdomen are drawn meeting, which is what the reference shows, and the",
    "hornet is the plate in this collection where the gap is the point.",
    "",
    "## Plate space",
    "",
    "y = 0 at the top of the head, y = 1000 at the tip of the abdomen. The wings",
    "reach past x = 830 and the hind tarsi below y = 1200."
  ],
